            "type": "written",
            "text": "Explain why there was so little progress in understanding the causes of disease during the Middle Ages. (12 marks)",
            "model": "The primary reason why medical understanding of disease causation remained stagnant during the medieval period was the absolute control of the Catholic Church over medical education. The Church maintained a strict monopoly on libraries, book production, and the copying of manuscripts by monks. This allowed them to rigorously promote the classical writings of Galen, whose theories of a balanced, purposeful body perfectly aligned with Christian theology.<br><br>Secondly, there was a profound lack of any scientific alternative to challenge the accepted classical ideas. Medieval physicians possessed no microscopes and had no concept of germs or microscopic pathogens. Without empirical evidence to the contrary, society universally accepted supernatural explanations, genuinely believing that illness was a divine punishment sent by God for sin, or the result of catastrophic planetary alignments.<br><br>Finally, this stagnation was reinforced by a culture that deeply revered tradition and discouraged original scientific inquiry. Universities taught medicine by reading aloud from ancient texts rather than encouraging physical experimentation or dissection. Even when dissections were performed to demonstrate Galen's anatomy, any inconsistencies found in the human body were blamed on the corpse being imperfect rather than Galen being wrong, ensuring that old ideas were preserved for centuries without question."
