            "type": "written",
            "text": "Explain why there was little change in medical treatments during the Renaissance period (c1500-c1700), despite new anatomical discoveries. (12 marks)",
            "model": "The primary reason why daily medical treatments remained stagnant was that the era's major anatomical and physiological breakthroughs had **no direct, practical clinical application** for treating sick patients. This clinical gap was clearly demonstrated by the work of **William Harvey**, who published ***De Motu Cordis* (1628)** proving that the heart acts as a muscular pump circulating blood in a one-way system around the body. While Harvey's discovery permanently disproved Galen's theory that the liver constantly manufactured new blood, it could not actively cure any diseases. **Consequently**, a physician in 1630 might fully accept Harvey's proof of blood circulation, but they still lacked the microscopic technology to see bacteria, understand infections, or perform safe blood transfusions (which would fail due to clotting and a lack of knowledge of blood groups). **This meant that** anatomical progress remained entirely separated from clinical capability, leaving doctors with no new curative tools.<br><br>Because scientific breakthroughs failed to yield new clinical cures, both professional physicians and the general public clung to the familiar **Theory of the Four Humours** as the only logical explanation for illness. Sickness continued to be diagnosed as an internal fluid imbalance, meaning patients expected and demanded traditional humoural treatments. **As a result**, university-trained physicians continued to prescribe **bleeding and purging** as their primary therapies. This clinical inertia was clearly demonstrated in **1685** when royal doctors gathered to treat the critically ill King Charles II. Despite living in the age of the Scientific Revolution, the King's physicians prescribed the same harmful bleeding, purging, and blistering techniques that had been used for centuries, demonstrating that classical humoural theory remained the dominant practical framework of medicine.<br><br>Furthermore, the stagnation of treatments was heavily reinforced by **the deep-seated conservatism of medical institutions and public attitudes**. Licensing bodies like the Royal College of Physicians strictly regulated who could practice medicine and frequently persecuted free-thinkers who attempted to abandon classical therapies. At the same time, the vast majority of ordinary citizens could not afford professional doctors and continued to rely on traditional, highly conservative healers. Peasants and townspeople still bought herbal remedies from **local apothecaries**, underwent bloodletting by **barber-surgeons**, or relied on female family members using home-made recipes passed down through generations. Because these local healers possessed no scientific training and operated within a traditional, bookless culture, their daily treatments remained completely untouched by the anatomical discoveries of Vesalius or Harvey, ensuring absolute continuity at the bedside."
