            "type": "written",
            "text": "Explain why the creation of the National Health Service (NHS) in 1948 improved public health in Britain. (12 marks)",
            "model": "The launch of the National Health Service (NHS) on 5 July 1948 led to an immediate and massive improvement in public health because it permanently removed the prohibitive financial barriers that had historically prevented the poorest sectors of society from accessing medical treatment. Prior to 1948, millions of working-class citizens, particularly women and children, could not afford GP consultations or hospital stays, relying instead on traditional home remedies or charitable voluntary hospitals of variable quality.<br><br>By nationalizing all British hospitals and providing healthcare that was entirely free at the point of delivery, funded through national taxation, the government ensured that medical care was distributed based on clinical need rather than wealth. This meant that the entire general population gained equal, universal access to trained General Practitioners (GPs), professional nursing, maternity care, and advanced surgeries, drastically reducing the number of people dying from preventable conditions.<br><br>Furthermore, the creation of a centralized NHS allowed the government to roll out highly successful, coordinated national preventative schemes. This infrastructure was crucial for delivering free childhood vaccination programs against diphtheria, whooping cough, and polio in the 1950s. These mass state interventions rapidly dropped infant mortality rates and significantly raised the nation's average life expectancy, transforming the health of the British population."
